      "writtenOn": "2021-08-20T13:43:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Is the jchar representation guaranteed to be null-terminated?\n\nI found some clues that it may not be: https://social.msdn.microsoft.com/Forums/sqlserver/en-US/320c789d-3068-4262-a73a-54bf7f7f9600/is-const-jchar-null-terminated-string\n\nIf that\u0027s the case, we need to get an explicit length with GetStringLength() and also write the conversion operator for std::u16string (to capture both the data and the length).\n\nTaking a step back, the reason we have CString is to allow access to the string chars as provided by JNI, without making an extra copy.\n\nIf for U16String we always have to make a std::u16string copy, then it would be simpler to just write an explicit conversion function instead of a RAII helper:\n\n  std::u16string JStringToU16String(JNIEnv* env, const jstring\u0026 jstr) {\n      const jchar* u16 \u003d env-\u003eGetStringChars(jstr, nullptr);\n      std::u16string str(u16, env-\u003eGetStringLength(jstr));\n      env-\u003eReleaseStringChars(jstr, u16);\n      return str;\n  }\n  \nThis can live in Text.cpp for now.",
      "revId": "89a46c51df7c04f2f233f0d7303039f1fb4dc54e",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
